  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n- Dry through Thursday night\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the region from the north\ntonight. The center of the high at the present time (18z) is over\nWestern Lake Superior. The high slowly moves south through\nWednesday night and will be centered over Lower Michigan Thursday\nmorning. The high then moves away to the southeast Thursday \nnight. Subsidence and a lack of deep moisture will allow us to \nkeep a dry forecast through Thursday night. Night time lows will \nbe in the 50s to lower 60s with day time highs in the low 80s. \nVery pleasant weather for mid summer.\n\n- Rain returns to the forecast for Friday through Saturday night \n\nThe high sliding away will allow moisture return to occur on the\nback side. BUFKIT overviews of moisture in the vertical are fairly\nstark with little to no moisture in place through Thursday night.\nOn Friday deep moisture surges back in through the depth of the\natmosphere. The deep moisture will be in place from Friday evening\nthrough Saturday evening. Our highest chances for precipitation\nwill be during this time frame (50-60 pct), but could see this \nneeding to be nudged up with time. \n\nA healthy mid level shortwave trough will swing through the region\nmoving directly overhead Friday night and Saturday. The surface\nreflection will be well south of our area with the low passing \nfrom Illinois into the Ohio Valley. If the low was in closer \nproximity we would have higher chances for rain. Given the south \ntrack of the low, moisture at the surface is not exceptional by \nmid summer standards. Low to mid 60s dew points are all we look \nto muster which results in limited MUCAPE/instability. MUCAPE \nvalues for the most part remain below 700 j/kg through the time \nthe trough is swinging through. There appears to be a brief window\nSunday morning where low level moisture lingers in the south \nwhere we may crest 1,000 j/kg. Bottom line the pattern looks \nconducive for occasional showers with a few embedded \nthunderstorms. Not expecting severe weather at this time. Dry \nweather returns for Sunday evening into early next week.\n\n- Temperatures near to slightly above normal through forecast\n\nTemperatures through the period look to be seasonal for the most\npart with highs in the 80s almost every day and lows for the most\npart in the 60s. We will see some 50s as well for lows. Normal\nhighs are low 80s and normal lows are around 60. 850mb\ntemperatures in the middle teens C lend credence to the forecast\ntemperatures.",
